4. Safe experiment: your own unit with ordering

This creates a harmless service that just logs a line. It only runs when you start it, and it doesn’t touch existing units.

bash
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/demo.service > /dev/null <<'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Demo ordering unit
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/bin/logger "demo.service ran after network-online"
RemainAfterExit=yes

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start demo.service
journalctl -u demo.service --no-pager
systemctl list-dependencies demo.service
systemd-analyze verify demo.service

Then experiment: change After= to Before=, run daemon-reload, and compare systemctl show demo.service -p After -p Before. Try sudo systemctl enable demo.service and see where the symlink lands (/etc/systemd/system/multi-user.target.wants/), which is exactly how “WantedBy” works.

Cleanup:

bash
sudo systemctl disable --now demo.service
sudo rm /etc/systemd/system/demo.service
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
What to avoid
systemctl isolate <target> on a desktop or remote machine. It stops everything not in that target and can kill your session.
systemctl set-default to something odd, since that changes what boots next time. If you want to practice it, set it and then set it right back.
mask, disable, or editing existing system units. Stick to your own demo unit.